  "summary": "Aneurin “Nye” Bevan (1897–1960) was a Welsh Labour politician who led the creation of Britain’s National Health Service and helped establish the welfare state. Born to a working-class family in Tredegar, he left school at 14 and became a coal miner and union activist. After leading local miners during the 1926 General Strike, he was elected MP for Ebbw Vale in 1929.\n\nAs Minister of Health in Clement Attlee’s government, Bevan drove the National Health Service Act through Parliament in 1946. The NHS began in 1948, providing medical care free at the point of need and bringing more than 2,500 hospitals into public ownership.\n\nBevan resigned from the cabinet in 1951 over proposed charges for dental and vision care and funding for rearmament. He later held senior Labour opposition roles and became the party’s deputy leader in 1959. He died of stomach cancer in 1960, aged 62.",
